From 385e7c735b7baeccdfa185a8df0b1cb220413f4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Prather-Huff <bryan@pratherhuff.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Jun 2026 22:54:57 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(cache): tolerate opaque (non-hash) upstream NAR URLs

When an upstream narinfo's URL: field is not hash-named (e.g. cachix
serves nar/<uuidv4>.nar.zst), ParseURL rejected it as an "invalid nar
hash" and the whole narinfo fetch aborted with HTTP 500. Nix treats a
500 as a hard error (worse than a 404), so this broke substitution for
every affected path once a fleet proxied a cachix-backed cache.

The Nix binary-cache protocol treats the narinfo URL: field as an opaque
path relative to the cache root, so a pull-through proxy must tolerate
non hash-named URLs rather than reject them. Decouple ncps's storage key
from the upstream URL string:

- pkg/nar: add ParseUpstreamURL(u, fallbackHash). Hash-named URLs behave
  exactly like ParseURL; opaque URLs preserve the original path for the
  upstream GET (new opaquePath field, used only by JoinURL) and key
  local storage off fallbackHash (the narinfo NarHash). Adds IsOpaque,
  OpaquePath and WithOpaquePath.
- ent: add nullable narinfos.upstream_url, persisting the opaque upstream
  path so the NAR can be re-fetched from upstream after local eviction.
- cache: pullNarInfo parses via ParseUpstreamURL (NarHash-derived key),
  re-serves the NAR under ncps's own hash-named URL, and persists the
  opaque path. lookupOriginalNarURL restores it for re-fetch;
  lookupPreferredUpstreamURL is made opaque-aware for CDC.

The hash-named fast path is unchanged, so this is purely additive.

Fixes bryandph/ncps#1

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
